;; Heretic mutation-testing config for shx. See docs/mutation-testing.md.
;;
;; Heretic is EXPERIMENTAL / pre-1.0 (upstream README: "a demo for the curious",
;; "not ready for production use"). It maps tests -> code via ClojureStorm, then
;; mutates source and re-runs only the covering tests. Run it with `bin/mutate`
;; or `bb mutate` (both wrap `clojure -M:heretic -m heretic.core ...`).
;;
;; Scope: the pure layers only — domain/law/shape. infra/ is file and process
;; I/O and cli/ is the process boundary; mutating them is slow and low-signal,
;; so they are neither instrumented (see the :heretic alias in deps.edn) nor
;; mutated here. A surviving mutant in a pure layer is a real test gap; a
;; surviving mutant in an I/O shim is usually just an untestable branch.

{:source-paths ["src"]
 :test-paths   ["test"]

 ;; :source-paths stays ["src"] so it matches the classpath root that the
 ;; coverage index records form paths against. Narrowing it to the pure
 ;; subdirectories instead would break that resolution, so scope is expressed
 ;; here: the uninstrumented namespaces are excluded from MUTATION, not just
 ;; from instrumentation.
 ;;
 ;; Why this matters: heretic scans every .clj under :source-paths for mutation
 ;; sites, but can only observe kills in instrumented namespaces. Left in, these
 ;; three files contributed 21 permanent "no coverage" sites — 41% of the report
 ;; — that no test could ever move, because instrumentation, not test quality,
 ;; is what makes them unobservable. A report that is 41% guaranteed-red is a
 ;; report agents learn to skim.
 ;;
 ;; Matching is by path SUFFIX. ADD ANY NEW infra/ OR PROCESS-BOUNDARY FILE
 ;; HERE, or it silently rejoins the no-coverage list forever. That cli and
 ;; infra have no tests at all is a real gap — it is tracked on the board as
 ;; `shx-bb-cli-untested`, which is the honest place for it.
 :exclude-files ["src/shx/cli.clj"
                 "src/shx/infra/config.clj"
                 "src/shx/infra/fs.clj"]

 ;; All five test namespaces stay in. shx.infra.config-test looks like it only
 ;; covers uninstrumented infra, but read-fragment-tree calls
 ;; shx.law.config/valid-config? on every fragment — so it is the only coverage
 ;; shx.law.config has. Excluding it would report law/config as no-coverage.
 :test-namespaces :all
 :exclude-test-namespaces #{}

 ;; Must match instrumentOnlyPrefixes in the :heretic alias. Never instrument
 ;; heretic/clojure.* themselves — ClojureStorm would recurse infinitely.
 :instrument-prefixes ["shx.domain" "shx.law" "shx.shape"]
 :instrument-skip-prefixes []

 ;; Coverage index + reports.
 :heretic-dir ".heretic"

 ;; Operators: :fast (16) · :minimal (31) · :standard (36, default) ·
 ;; :comprehensive (81). Standard until the survivor list is triaged to zero;
 ;; raise it then, because a preset raised over an untriaged list just buries
 ;; the old survivors in new ones.
 :preset :standard
 :skip-forms #{comment}

 ;; Per-mutant test timeout. shx has no slow paths — the whole suite is
 ;; sub-second — so the 5s default would be generous. 10s is headroom for a
 ;; mutant that turns a fold into a non-terminating one.
 :timeout-ms 10000

 ;; mutate/watch run in a disposable copy of the tree; the working tree is only
 ;; ever READ. The child JVM reuses this alias for the ClojureStorm classpath.
 ;; Keeping the sandbox lets rsync make the next run incremental.
 :sandbox-aliases ["heretic"]
 :sandbox-dir ".heretic-sandbox"
 :keep-sandbox true

 :report-format :terminal
 :output-path "target/heretic-report"}
